WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.234
-Jesse Williams, welcome.

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.400
Welcome to the show.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.734
-Thank you.

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:06.067
Um, wow.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:07.567
-Yeah. I took it off.

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:08.801
-You're a brave man.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:11.067
I would feel like a naked
mole rat if I did that.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.234
[ Laughter ]
-Would you really?

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:14.067
-It does not suit me. No.

00:00:14.067 --> 00:00:15.467
But you're wearing it well.

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:18.434
-Well, I think you are
a very sexy man.

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:19.767
[ Laughter ]
You can do any--

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:21.767
-That escalated quickly, yeah.
-You can do any --

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:23.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:25.901
You can do anything with that.
I have -- yeah.

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:27.567
-I need something.
-Yeah. No, I don't.

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:29.400
I tried that full thing
and now I don't know.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.234
I just go to extremes.
But, yeah, I'm happy.

00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:33.400
I'm happy to be sitting

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.467
next to such a handsome man
after such a change.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.400
-Wow.
Nice little live change.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.567
It's kind of like Broadway.

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.133
You're just out here,
just risking it all.

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.334
-Yeah, exactly,
just risking it all.

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:43.167
Yeah, just do it.
Hey, by the way, I just heard

00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.367
that you got cast in

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:46.033
"Only Murders in the Building"
season three.

00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:47.434
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:48.601
Yeah, I'm excited.

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:49.767
-What do you know about that?

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.267
-I don't -- I don't know a lot.

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:52.767
We actually have a table read
next week.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:54.534
I'm -- I'm over the moon.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:56.000
I mean, Martin Short,
Steve Martin.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.033
It's...
-Come on.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.634
-I grew up, like,
sneaking down the stairs

00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:00.968
to watch them
on "Saturday Night Live"

00:01:00.968 --> 00:01:03.167
over my mom's shoulder.
Like, that was their --

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:04.434
-Really?
-Absolutely.

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:06.434
-Was TV a big thing for you
growing up?

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:08.100
-We were not really allowed
to watch TV.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.734
Well, we had a system.
It's actually quite admirable.

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:12.400
I think about it now,
as a parent.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.734
My mom would cut like four
cardboard squares

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.567
that represented a half hour
of television,

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:20.067
and you get four half hour
blocks of television a week.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:21.934
And you put them in a jar,
and when you use them --

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:23.267
You can use them
whenever you want.

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.634
I used them for Saturday morning
cartoons, pretty much.

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:27.534
And like, then when
"The Simpsons" came out,

00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:28.701
like, "The Simpsons."

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.567
But I was not allowed
to watch "SNL,"

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:33.133
but I would, like,
sneak down the stairs

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.634
and my mom always watched --
my parents always watched "SNL."

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.734
And I would get the bonus,
and I would watch, you know,

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:39.467
Ed Grimley and
the synchronized swimming

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.801
and Wild and Crazy Guys,
and now I get to work with them.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.267
So, I'm excited.
-That's what it's all about.

00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.601
-I'm pretty excited.
-That's great. I love that.

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:46.767
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:49.234
-A lot of people know you
from "Grey's Anatomy."

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:50.601
-Sure.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:53.934
-And do people, when you're
doing Broadway, do they --

00:01:53.934 --> 00:01:56.100
do they say hi to you
after the show and like,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.434
"Hey, we just came because
we're 'Grey's Anatomy' fans"?

00:01:58.434 --> 00:01:59.834
-There's a lot of "Grey's" fans
at the stage door.

00:01:59.834 --> 00:02:01.300
It's kind of a...

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.033
One cool thing, I think, is that
it's bringing a lot of folks

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:06.033
from all over the world,
surprisingly,

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:08.300
that have never seen
a Broadway play,

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.634
coming to see it because
they're big fans of "Grey's."

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:12.868
And, you know, we have fans
that are 12 and 72.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:16.133
I mean, we have the whole -- we
have four generations of folks,

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:18.400
primarily women,
who come and see the --

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.234
[ Laughter ]
...come and see the show.

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:22.400
And they come, "Yeah, I'm from
Chile, and we love the show,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.968
and we just wanted to come."
Because they know there,

00:02:23.968 --> 00:02:25.634
they're going to see you
in real life.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:27.100
-They know that they can
stand there...

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.534
I don't want to solicit this,
because it's a lot of people.

00:02:30.534 --> 00:02:31.834
But, like, they can stand
in one spot

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:33.334
and I'm going to be there
every night.

00:02:33.334 --> 00:02:34.834
And so, it's a pretty cool
experience for them.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.467
And it's a hell of a show,
honestly.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:38.434
So, I'm excited about it.
-And you're back doing it.

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:40.267
I mean, this is --
-Yeah, that was not expected.

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:41.901
-It takes so much work, by
the way, to do a Broadway play.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:44.367
I mean, it's just --
any Broadway play,

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.567
but especially what you're doing
in this role.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:47.834
It's unbelievable.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.267
Did you have a second thought
where you're like,

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.868
"I don't know if I -- if I can
physically can come back

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:53.133
and do this"?
-Well, I mean,

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:56.501
when we were almost done
the first run earlier this year,

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:58.334
they came to my dressing room.

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.133
I remember, our director,
Scott Ellis --

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.133
incredible director -- came
to our dressing room and asked,

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.100
you know, do we --
It was probably May.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.467
"Do you want to" -- I mean,
they're talking about

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.267
extending it, because it was
a big success.

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:08.567
It went really well.
-Yeah.

00:03:08.567 --> 00:03:11.601
-And I -- It's such an
emotionally exhausting role.

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.767
It's just so -- It just
beat the hell out of me.

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:15.767
I was just like, "No, get out
of my dressing room."

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.467
-Yeah, you're like,
"I cannot do this."

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:18.567
-Like, "No, I'm tired.
I'm tired."

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:20.067
I want-- I could not wait to --

00:03:20.067 --> 00:03:21.901
I loved it, but I need a --
-Exactly.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.067
-It was beating the hell
out of me.

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:24.534
I got a summer with my kids.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:26.868
I got to kind of decompress,
get some perspective.

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:29.534
And it was just still in me.
I was running the lines.

00:03:29.534 --> 00:03:31.701
I was still -- It was just --
You carry it with you.

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:33.534
-In the summer?
-Yeah, throughout.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:35.234
I was still running the lines,
working around the house,

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:36.534
messing around, and I just --

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:39.501
We left on such a high, I knew
we had more in the tank.

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:40.901
And we just -- Well, how rare --

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.701
Like you just said,
it's such a rare opportunity.

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:44.100
-Yeah.
-Once in a lifetime

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.601
to be a lead in a Broadway play
with incredible actors.

00:03:46.601 --> 00:03:48.767
This company is so fantastic.

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:50.234
When do you get a chance
to do that again?

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:52.033
That's not promised.
-But also, here's...

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:53.267
Here's your Little League team.

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:55.100
I just want to take a look
with you.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.100
-[ Laughs ]
-Yeah, there you are there.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.267
[ Audience awws ]
-Ahh.

00:03:58.267 --> 00:03:59.934
-And now, here you are
wearing a uniform.

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:02.334
-I was tan, man.
I had a little color back then.

00:04:02.334 --> 00:04:04.834
-But here you are wearing
a uniform on Broadway.

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:06.601
-Yeah. Yeah.
-And did you ever think

00:04:06.601 --> 00:04:07.934
this kid -- Were you ever like,

00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:09.434
"Oh, my gosh,
I'm gonna be playing" --

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.200
-I think at that point
I thought

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.534
I could play
in the major leagues.

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:13.868
-Yeah, real big.
I'm gonna be a real --

00:04:13.868 --> 00:04:15.701
Not an actor baseball player.
-I was deluded, yeah.

00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:18.000
And then second to that,
I'll be a fake player

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.334
or a real player, was the hope.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:22.834
-Well, can we talk just about --
What is "Take Me Out"?

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:24.067
Can you tell everyone
what it's about?

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:26.601
-Yeah, "Take Me Out,"
it follows essentially,

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:28.434
kind of like
the New York Yankees.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.501
It is the best player on the
best team,

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:33.534
championship-winning team,
at the height of his career,

00:04:33.534 --> 00:04:35.834
with everything ahead of him.

00:04:35.834 --> 00:04:37.133
He's on top of the world.

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:42.133
And as a surprise to everyone,
he comes out as gay.

00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:43.968
Doesn't warn anybody.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:47.734
And the play begins just in
the immediate aftermath of that.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:50.901
And it's just an examination
of male relationships

00:04:50.901 --> 00:04:52.267
after a word is said.

00:04:52.267 --> 00:04:56.167
Because nothing behaviorally
changes about this player.

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:58.534
He's the alpha on the team,
the alpha in the league.

00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:02.200
He doesn't -- there's no --
there's no kind of affectation

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.200
or gimmick for men
to hide behind

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.701
with their, like, homophobia
and the disorientation around.

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:09.300
It's not like, "Well, it's cool,
but I wish he didn't do this"

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.968
or "I wish he didn't
talk like that," or "I wish" --

00:05:10.968 --> 00:05:12.300
I'm not doing anything.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.300
It's just how men struggle
to handle a relationship,

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.601
and what is a relationship
if you can't be your true self

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:20.300
in front of each other?
And it's...

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.634
And there's a lot of growth,

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.300
and it's very much
an homage to baseball.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:26.367
And I grew up playing baseball,
you know, year-round.

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:28.567
It was very, very sacred
in our household

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:30.767
and a huge bonding experience
for me and my father.

00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:33.000
-Me too.
-I definitely --

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.634
I definitely was conscious of
doing -- choosing to do the role

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:38.300
as part of an homage to my dad
and, like, our relationship.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.534
-Did he get to see you
in the role?

00:05:39.534 --> 00:05:41.200
-He's been to two --
twice already.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.367
And he's coming --
-Really?

00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:43.501
-Yeah, he's coming again.
-What does he think?

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:45.334
-Yeah. He loved it.
He loved it.

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:47.834
And, you know,
he complimented the swing.

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:49.167
You know, he approved.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:51.334
He approved of the mechanics.
-He liked your stance?

00:05:51.334 --> 00:05:52.334
He's like, "Yeah,
I taught you well."

00:05:52.334 --> 00:05:53.434
-He's a mechanics guy.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:54.801
And, but no, he loved it.

00:05:54.801 --> 00:05:57.767
And it's --
it's a hell of a play.

00:05:57.767 --> 00:05:59.267
It's really, really compelling.
So, I'm proud of it.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:01.434
-You're fantastic in it,
and I'm happy that you're here.

00:06:01.434 --> 00:06:02.767
Good luck in season three.
-Thank you so much.

00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:03.767
-...of "Only Murders
in the Building."

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:04.767
-Thank you.
I appreciate it.

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:05.767
-Come back, please.
-Thank you, man.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:07.434
-Jesse Williams!
"Take Me Out" is playing

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:10.400
at the Schoenfeld Theatre
through February 5th.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.734
We'll be right back
with stand-up from Mary Mack.

00:06:12.734 --> 00:06:13.801
Stick around, everybody.

